## Rendered states (textual description — no live daemon with the ADR 0023
backend changes was reachable this session; same honest fallback used by
the ADR-0021 and ADR-0022 frontend rounds, not a fabricated screenshot claim)

**Delegation-authorization prompt, pending.** Below the flat step list (or
fan-out lanes, if the pause occurred inside a plan branch), an inline,
non-modal card appears: "`researcher` wants to send work to this agent —
{description from its Agent Card}", a trust badge (`✓ trusted` /
`○ untrusted`) + provenance line, and three buttons: "Don't send this run"
(ghost), "Authorize this run" (primary), "Always authorize" (secondary). The
run header simultaneously shows "◉ Awaiting your decision" in place of the
pulsing "Kelter working…" badge — identical treatment to a skill-activation
pause, since the mechanics (pause the axis, wait on a human, resume via a
paired WS event) are the same even though the stakes differ.

**Delegation-authorization prompt, resolving.** Clicking a button disables
all three and shows a spinner on the clicked one (`loading` prop, same
`Button` component as everywhere else); the prompt does not disappear until
the paired `agent_delegation_decided` WS event clears
`axis.pendingDelegation` — no local optimistic dismiss, matching the skill
prompt's contract exactly (a network failure mid-decision leaves the prompt
visibly still pending, with an inline error line, rather than silently
vanishing).

**A2A lane with a real trust badge.** A fan-out lane's header row now reads,
e.g., "⇄ delegate  ✓ trusted" or "⇄ delegate  ○ untrusted" — same visual
weight and token as the skill-activation prompt's own trust badge (success/
quiet tone respectively). A lane whose action doesn't resolve to any
registered agent (e.g. the `/api/v1/agents` fetch failed, or the daemon
predates agent registration entirely) shows only the kind badge, no trust
badge — an honest omission, not a fabricated "untrusted" default.

**Settings — Agents panel.** Below Skills (own divider), a "Agents" heading:
a policy selector identical in shape to Skills' (three options, ask reveals
fallback + timeout sub-fields), a "Register by card URL" form (URL + optional
token-env-var field, disclosure copy explaining new registrations start
untrusted), inline save/register status line, and a "Registered" list of
cards (name, description, trust badge, source_kind/source line, Remove with
inline confirm). Statically-configured agents (source_kind `"config"`,
default-trusted per the design doc's split) and dynamically-registered ones
(source_kind `"discovery"`, default-untrusted) render through the exact same
list — the split is visible only via the badge + source line, never a
separate section, matching the design doc's framing that both are the "same
kind of act, spelled differently."

## Trust badge sourcing (a genuinely new frontend decision, not
contract-specified — flagged for Eryk in `07` D-12)

The design doc's Part 5 contract adds a `trust` field only to the new
`agent_delegation_requested` ask-pause event — NOT to the ordinary
`proposed`/`executed` events every a2a lane already renders through. Under
the default `TrustedOnly` policy (no ask pause at all for a trusted agent),
a lane's event stream therefore carries no trust data whatsoever. Rather
than inventing a new field on `proposed`/`executed` the backend never
promised (which would risk exactly the kind of contract mismatch the brief
warned about), the badge is sourced by joining the lane's `action`
(`delegate_to_<sanitized>`) against `GET /api/v1/agents` (Part 3, a route
the contract DOES fix) — sanitizing every fetched agent's `name` the same
way the a2a crate does when it builds that very action name in the first
place (read directly from `crates/a2a/src/lib.rs:411-422`, not inferred).
This mapping is deliberately forward-only: sanitization is lossy (two
distinct names could theoretically collide), so the code never attempts to
reverse an action suffix back into an exact name — it only ever tests
"does this known agent's name sanitize to this suffix." No match → no
badge, never a fabricated default.

