## P4 — incomplete, honest gap (STOP condition invoked as instructed)

At the point I reached P4, `crates/kernel-core`, `crates/a2a`, and
`crates/api` contained **zero** matches for `AgentDelegationRequested`,
`AgentRegistry`, `DelegationGate`, `ResilienceConfig`, or `circuit_state` —
P4's backend work (and P3's registry/gate work too) had not started. I
messaged `agent-trust-backend-impl` directly asking for its current state
and, if it hadn't started yet, what shape a retry attempt takes on the
`RunEvent` stream and whether a circuit-open refusal is literally just a
`Rejected` event with a descriptive reason. I did not receive a reply and no
matching backend code landed by the time I finished everything else in
scope (re-checked immediately before writing this log).

**What I could still verify without the backend, and did:** the
circuit-open-refusal question is answered by the design doc itself (Part 4:
"An OPEN circuit for a candidate is exactly another kind of refusal in this
same loop, handled identically [to a budget/depth/cycle refusal]") combined
with the P1+P2 round's own established precedent (its evidence log states
plainly: "a governance-refused delegation is therefore a completely
ordinary `StepCard`... same danger chip, same copy shape... only the reason
string differs" — already implemented, already shipped, unchanged by this
round). A circuit-open refusal is exactly this shape by the design doc's own
account, so **no new frontend code is needed for it** — it composes for
free through the existing `Rejected`/`StepCard` rendering. I did not add any
new UI vocabulary for it, per the design doc's own instruction not to invent
one unless the existing rendering "genuinely can't represent it" — it can.

**What remains genuinely ungrounded:** whether a retry attempt (as opposed
to a fresh first attempt) is distinguishable on the event stream at all —
its own `Proposed`/`Executed` pair with an `attempt`-like field, or a silent
replacement of the prior attempt's outcome. This determines whether a
"retrying…" visual state (StepCard already has one, currently only reachable
via the unrelated `rejections.length > 0` condition — see `StepCard.tsx`'s
`state` computation) is reachable for a governed retry, or whether retries
are invisible to the UI beyond the final outcome. **I did not guess this.**
No code was written or changed for retry-attempt visualization. This is the
one open item from the full P3+P4 scope; it needs either backend code to
read once it exists, or direct coordination with `agent-trust-backend-impl`
before it can be closed. Flagging it here explicitly rather than silently
shipping an assumption.

## Judgment calls (for Eryk)

- **`POST /api/v1/runs/{id}/delegation` endpoint path is an analogy, not a
  contract-fixed name.** The design doc's Part 5 contract fixes the new
  `RunEvent` variants and `RunStatus` value but — unlike D-9's original
  brief, which named `/api/v1/runs/{id}/skill-activation` explicitly — does
  not name the per-run decide route for delegation. I chose
  `/api/v1/runs/{id}/delegation` with body `{agent, decision}` by direct
  structural analogy to the skill-activation route. **Verify this against
  whatever `agent-trust-backend-impl` actually names it** and correct the
  one call site in `src/store/app.ts::decideDelegation` if it differs — a
  single-line fix, isolated on purpose.
- **`AgentPolicyView.policy` wire values assumed `"trusted"`/`"all"`/`"ask"`**
  (not `"trusted_only"`), by direct analogy to `SkillsPolicyView.model_activation`,
  which uses the same three literal values despite its Rust enum being named
  `ModelActivationPolicy::TrustedOnly` (grepped `crates/skills/src/gate.rs`:
  the wire mapping is a manual `.as_str()`-style method, not a
  `#[serde(rename_all)]` auto-derive, and it deliberately maps `TrustedOnly`
  → `"trusted"`). Since the design doc states `AgentDelegationPolicy` "mirrors
  `ModelActivationPolicy` exactly," the same wire convention was assumed.
  Verify against the actual backend enum's wire mapping once it exists.
- **Trust badge sourcing (client-side join) — see the dedicated section
  above.** Called out again here because it's the largest surface-area
  judgment call in this round and the one most likely to need revisiting if
  the backend later adds a `trust` field directly to `proposed`/`executed`.
- Did not add a Settings-side "resilience config" or "circuit breaker status"
  view — the design doc's Part 5 Requirements section only asks for
  visibility of circuit-open/retry states IN A LANE, not a standalone
  operator panel, and no HTTP surface for resilience config was specified in
  Part 3's fixed routes list. Left out rather than speculatively added.

## P4 addendum — grounded against the shipped backend (2026-07-20, later same session)

`agent-trust-backend-impl` replied with the actual `RunEvent::Rejected`
shape and `run_delegation_resilience`'s behavior after finishing tasks
#84-89. This closes the one gap left open above. Full reasoning is in
`design/03-screens/R-3-live-timeline.md`'s new "Governed resilience" section
(ADR 0023 P4); summary here:

- **No new UI vocabulary needed for retry/fallback attempts or circuit-open
  refusals mid-loop** — every intermediate failure is an ordinary `Rejected`
  on the step's existing index; only the loop's final outcome gets the
  step's one `Executed`. `StepCard`'s existing `rejections` list and
  `retrying…` state already render this, unmodified.
- **Two real bugs found and fixed, only visible once the actual wire shape
  was known:** `Rejected.attempt` is 1-based per phase (retry vs fallback
  count independently from 1), so `attempt: 1` legitimately repeats for one
  step. `events.ts`'s dedupe (`attempt` alone) would have silently dropped
  the second phase's real rejection — fixed to key on `attempt + reason`.
  `StepCard.tsx`'s React list `key={r.attempt}` would have collided on the
  same repeat — fixed to key on array index.
- **One case where "composes for free" was false, found by verifying
  rather than assuming:** a circuit already open before the very first,
  fresh dial produces no `Rejected` at all — it's folded into the step's
  terminal `Executed{ok:false, result:{"error": "..."}}}`. Since
  `{"error": "..."}` is confirmed (grepped `engine.rs`) to be the kernel's
  general failed-result convention, not delegation-specific, `StepCard` now
  extracts and always shows that string inline for any `executed-failed`
  step, rather than leaving it behind a click-to-expand — the smallest fix
  that restores the "never hidden" rejection-visibility convention for this
  one path.
- **Endpoint-path judgment call confirmed correct:** `POST
  /api/v1/runs/{id}/delegation` — the backend built the identical route
  independently (`crates/api/src/lib.rs:353`).
- `pnpm --dir apps/web-ui build` re-confirmed clean after all of the above
  (`tsc -b` strict + `vite build`, 58 modules, no new dependency).
- Plan-mode (fan-out lane) delegation resilience is explicitly out of scope
  this round per the backend (no in-node retry/fallback loop; a circuit-open
  or gate refusal at plan-mode's admission point is a plain `Rejected` at
  the node's `branch`, already covered by existing fan-out lane rendering
  with zero changes needed) — a disclosed backend scope decision, not a
  frontend gap.

